  "course_key": "g3-sec2-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec2-s1",
  "position": 6,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Estimated mean for grouped data",
  "summary": "Use class midpoints and frequencies to estimate a mean, solve missing-frequency problems and judge the limits of the estimate.",
  "outcomes": [
    "1.10"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "s1-lesson-09"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 60,
  "objectives": [
    "Find class midpoints and construct frequency products.",
    "Calculate an estimated grouped mean using Σfx/Σf.",
    "Use a grouped-mean equation to find an unknown frequency."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "s1-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Represent each class by its midpoint",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Grouped data do not reveal individual observations, so use each class midpoint as a representative value. The midpoint is the average of the lower and upper class boundaries. Multiply it by the class frequency to estimate that class's contribution to the total. In the table, the class 10 to 20 means 10 ≤ t < 20: it includes 10 but not 20, so each value belongs to exactly one class. The columns are the midpoint x, the frequency f and their product fx."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "s1-lesson-10-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-explanation-2",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Apply the weighted-mean formula",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Add every midpoint-frequency product to obtain Σfx, then divide by total frequency Σf. Keep the numerator and denominator visible to reduce transcription errors. Include every class even if its midpoint is zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-explanation-3",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Interpret the result as an estimate",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The answer is estimated because observations may not actually equal their class midpoints. Narrower classes usually reduce possible approximation error, but only the raw data give the exact mean. For an unknown frequency, place the variable in both Σfx and Σf before solving."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-example-1",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: calculate a grouped mean",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use the table in the diagram to estimate the mean time."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Multiply each midpoint by its frequency and add."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\Sigma fx=15(4)+25(7)+35(3)=60+175+105=340"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\Sigma f=4+7+3=14"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{estimated mean}=\\frac{340}{14}\\approx24.3\\text{ minutes}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The estimate lies between the smallest and largest midpoints, 15 and 35, and closer to 25, where most of the data are."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find a missing frequency",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Midpoints 10, 20 and 30 have frequencies 3, f and 5. The estimated mean is 22. Find f."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\frac{10(3)+20f+30(5)}{3+f+5}=22"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "180+20f=22(8+f)=176+22f"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "4=2f\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad f=2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "With f = 2, the total frequency is 10 and Σfx = 30 + 40 + 150 = 220, so the mean is 220 ÷ 10 = 22."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-recall",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Grouped data have midpoints 6 and 18 with frequencies 3 and 1. Find the estimated mean."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "9",
        "canonical_latex": "9",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Σfx = 6(3) + 18(1) = 36 and Σf = 4, so the estimated mean is 36 ÷ 4 = 9."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-10-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Estimated mean = Σfx ÷ Σf, using each class midpoint as x."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find each midpoint, add an fx column, divide the two totals, and remember that the result is an estimate. For a missing frequency, put the unknown into both Σfx and Σf."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
